{
  "teamId": 114,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Cleveland Guardians opened their season with a familiar brand of baseball — pitching-driven, scrappy, and built on moments rather than muscle. An 88-74 record and a Wild Card berth told the story of a team that found ways to win when it mattered, even if the margin for error was razor-thin all year long. With an OPS of just .669 and runs scored barely edging below their runs allowed, Cleveland lived and died by the big swing at the right moment.\n\nThe early weeks of the season were defined by unexpected heroes stepping into the spotlight. Daniel Schneemann's grand slam on May 3rd was perhaps the single most impactful offensive moment of the first half, a stunning +0.784 WPA swing that announced Cleveland's depth was real. Carlos Santana provided veteran pop, and Kyle Manzardo emerged as a legitimate middle-of-the-order threat. These Spring contributions laid the foundation for a team that refused to be counted out.\n\nThe summer months brought turbulence. Emmanuel Clase, the ironclad closer, suffered a brutal blown save in late July when Jonathan India took him deep for a three-run homer — a -0.707 gut punch that exposed Cleveland's vulnerability in high-leverage situations. Cade Smith repeatedly showed up on the wrong side of the ledger, surrendering timely hits that cost the Guardians dearly. But every time the team seemed to wobble, someone answered — Nolan Jones, Brayan Rocchio, and the emerging CJ Kayfus all delivered in September to push Cleveland across the finish line and into the postseason.\n\nThe Wild Card round against Detroit was a tense, grinding affair spread across three games. José Ramírez's hustle single in Game 1 nearly broke things open, only for a botched play at the plate to kill the rally. Brayan Rocchio homered in Game 2 to keep hope alive, but the Guardians' thin offense ultimately couldn't sustain the fight. Kerry Carpenter tortured Cleveland's bullpen across multiple games, and a late multi-run single off Hunter Gaddis in Game 3 sealed Cleveland's fate. The 2025 Guardians bowed out in October, a hard-luck end to a season defined more by grit than glory.",
